    <statement><p>Find the horizontal asymptote, if it exists, of the rational function below.  If the function does not have a horizontal asymptote, enter <em>NONE.</em></p>
<p><m>\displaystyle \ g(x) =  \frac{(-1 - x)(-7  - 2 x)}{2 x^2 + 1}</m></p>
<p>The horizontal asymptote has equation <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>y = 1</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><alert>  SOLUTION </alert>



<me>g(x) = \frac{(-1 - x)(-7  - 2 x)}{2 x^2 + 1} = -\frac{-2 x^2}{2 x^2} = -\frac{-2}{2}
\quad\mbox{as}\quad x \to \pm \infty.</me>
Therefore <m>g(x)</m> has a horizontal asymptote at
<me>y = -\frac{-2}{2}.</me>



</solution>
